# {{name}}

Generated by `pnpm turbo gen remote` in **standalone mode** — an independent
project, outside the `enterprise-microfrontend-boilerplate` monorepo, that an
orchestrating shell composes over the network.

**It depends on no package from that monorepo.** Everything it needs from its
host arrives as props (`src/internal/contract.ts` declares their shape), so
`pnpm install` works from the public registry with no token and no private
registry setup. Move this directory into its own repository and it keeps
building.

## Running it

Standalone, with no shell present — a remote is a portable application in
its own right:

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm dev      # http://localhost:{{port}}
pnpm build
```

With no host to supply them, `session` and `bus` come from
`src/internal/standalone-host.ts`: a signed-out session and a bus local to the
page. In production the orchestrator supplies both.

## Composing it inside a shell

This project's own generator has no registry it owns to write to —
register this remote in a consuming shell's own remote registry by adding
an entry shaped like:

```jsonc
{
  "name": "{{name}}",
  "entry": "https://<where-this-remote-is-deployed>/mf-manifest.json",
  "routePath": "{{routePath}}",
  "label": "{{title}}"
}
```

The shell must also allow this remote's origin — see "Adding a remote you
host elsewhere" in the source monorepo's `docs/USAGE.md`.

## The contract with the host

`src/exposed/App.tsx` receives:

| Prop | What it is |
|---|---|
| `basePath` | The route the orchestrator mounted this remote at. |
| `session` | Who is signed in, already resolved by the orchestrator. |
| `bus` | `publish` / `subscribe` for messaging between remotes. |

`bus` payloads are `unknown` on purpose: the publisher is a different build,
so validate what you receive before using it.

If the orchestrator widens the contract, copy the addition into
`src/internal/contract.ts`. That file is the only thing to keep in sync.

## Structure

The same `src/exposed/` / `src/internal/` split every remote in the source
monorepo uses:

```text
src/
├── exposed/
│   └── App.tsx               # what a shell mounts ("{{name}}/App")
├── internal/
│   ├── contract.ts           # the props the host passes in
│   ├── standalone-host.ts    # stand-ins for those props in dev
│   ├── tokens.css            # this app's own design tokens
│   └── placeholder/          # replace with this remote's real feature
├── bootstrap.tsx             # standalone dev entry
└── index.tsx                 # dynamic import('./bootstrap') — the MF async boundary
```

## What's deliberately not here

- No real feature — this is placeholder content, structurally correct and
  ready to build on.
- No design-system package — bring your own, or keep extending
  `internal/tokens.css`.
- No CI/CD — set that up for wherever this project actually gets deployed.
- No production remote registration — that is a deployment decision for
  whichever shell composes this remote, not something a scaffolding tool
  should do automatically.
